Pandora Ecosystems

Elevation		Vegetation Zone		Primary Characteristics
0 – 3,000 ft		Lowland Jungle		Massive broad-leafed trees, high humidity, active carnivores.
3,000 - 5,000 ft	The Cloud Transition	Permanent mist; transition from "predatory" to "passive" flora.
5,000 - 6,000 ft	The Iron-Gate		Steep cliffs; sudden appearance of giant "Redwood-style" titans.
6,000 – 9,000ft		Highland Forest		Trees like oaks and ferns; cooler temperatures.
9,000 – 12,000ft	Alpine Grasslands	High-UV, grasses, frost-resistant succulents, no trees.
12,000+ ft		High Desert / Ice	Bare rock, lichens, permanent or seasonal snow caps.

* On smaller islands cut the elevation zone heights by half.


A. Jungle
   (1) Jungle Floor (0ft to 40ft, ~1% of sunlight): This is the domain of Mega-Arthropods, Giant Tank Crabs, and large carnivorous plants that are hidden primarily underground. During the daylight the jungle floor is a realm of deep shadow, with very little direct sunlight making its way through the multi-layered canopy above. Only around 1% of sunlight typically penetrates the dense canopy to reach the jungle floor, making it a dim and shadowed environment described as twilight, even at midday. During the night the jungle floor comes to life with bioluminescent plants that give a fantastic other-worldly feeling when seen. The jungle floor is characterized by sometimes massive tree trunks, a chaotic mix of ferns & flowers, giant insects, and hidden carnivorous plants. Almost everything that dies in the upper regions of the jungle ends up in this region and it a true representation of death and new life.
   "Descending to the Jungle Floor is like entering the lightless hull of a sunken ship. Here, the 'Green Roar' of the canopy is muffled by a crushing, humid silence, broken only by the wet squelch of 'Mud Lotus' and the distant, rhythmic clicking of 'Tank Crabs' patrolling the shadows. The air is thick, tasting of rich ozone and ancient decay—a 'Grave-Scent' that marks the end of all things. In the permanent twilight, the massive, buttressed trunks of the Banyan-oaks rise like the pillars of a forgotten tomb, their roots snaking through a chaotic carpet of 'Slumbermoss' and 'Reachy Gushrooms.' It is a world of slow, heavy pressure, where the only 'stars' are the cold, flickering bioluminescent pulses of 'Lactis Combibo' waiting in the dark. To walk here is to move through the jungle's belly; a place where every step is a negotiation with the hidden mouths of the earth."
      Environment: Very high humidity, much cooler temperature compared to the canopy, very little airflow.
      Flora: Ferns, Mosses, Lichens, Liverworts, Fungi
      Fauna: Armored Insects, Large Tank Crabs
      Hostile Fauna: Groundmouth, Slumbermoss, Caro Vorago Carnivorous, Lactis Combibo Carnivorous, Muliebris Crusta Carnivorous, Mud Lotus Carnivorous, Reachy Gushroom, Creamy Mushroom, 

   (2) Under-Canopy (10ft to 60ft, ~5% of sunlight): While the jungle floor is virtually chaos incarnate, the under-canopy is a fairly open region where massive tree limbs stretch out from oak-banyan trees, forming a labyrinthine network stretching upward. Between the limbs of the oak-banyan trees are the trunks of even larger Baobab-Dipterocarp trees and occasional Ceiba trees. A large number of parasitic fig-like vines reach down to this area to anchor to the larger trees, as do many large carnivorous plants that use lures and strangling vines to trap prey above and below this region.
   "Descending from the highway into the Under-Canopy is like stepping behind the curtain of a great machine. Here, the vibrant chaos of the leaf-mat gives way to a grim, architectural grey. The air grows still and heavy, smelling of damp bark and stagnant sap. This is the 'Vascular Vault'—a shadowy gallery of vertical trunks and massive, bridge-like limbs that hold the weight of the world aloft. Between these colossal pillars, the jungle’s 'plumbing' is exposed: thick, pulsating vines hang like elevator cables, while the 'Mawhives' and 'Chamelius' plants dangle their lures in the gloom like bioluminescent lanterns. It is a zone of eerie, structural tension; a place where you are no longer a resident, but a passenger moving through the ribs of a giant, breathing engine."
      Environment: High humidity, cooler temperature compared to the lower canopy, and almost no wind compared to the upper canopy.
      Flora: Mosses, Orchids, Lichens, Bromeliads, small trees, shrubs
      Fauna: Large insects, Tree Snails
      Hostile Fauna: Paradartus Carnivorous, Acidus Emanio Carnivorous, Chamelius Carnivorous, Leech Melonus Carnivorous, Slimewarticus Carnivorous, Mawhive, 

   (3) Lower Canopy (60ft to 140ft, 50% sunlight): Within the lower-canopy the massive lateral limbs of the Banyan-oaks have woven themselves into an interconnected highway where most of the life in the jungle can be found, at least some of the time. Larger arboreal animals live within this region almost exclusively, and smaller arboreal animals often use this region to move from one part of the upper canopy to another. Throughout the region are numerous carnivorous plants of nearly every type, from plants with grasping tendrils to plants that use lures and wait for animals to climb within to be devoured. The entire region of the lower canopy is so full of sound and color that it can be overwhelming to creatures from above & below the region.
   "Step into the Lower Canopy and you step into the 'Green Roar.' This is the jungle’s frantic, multi-lane highway—a sprawling, horizontal labyrinth where the massive lateral limbs of the Banyan-oaks have fused into a solid, undulating pavement. The air is thick with the musk of a thousand species and the cloying, fermented sweetness of competing lures. Sunlight filters through in blinding, dappled shafts, illuminating a riot of neon-colored orchids and the constant, blurring motion of primates and gliders. It is a realm of sensory overload; a vibrant, crowded intersection where the only constant is competition, and the silence of the layers above and below is replaced by the deafening, rhythmic heartbeat of the living world."
      Enviroment: High humidity, stable temperatures, and little wind compared to the upper canopy.
      Flora: Angel Oak/Banyon trees, Kapok Tree, Moreton Bay Fig, Rubber trees (These trees grow upward very fast to around 100ft and then spread outward, with very old trees reaching out as mush as 100ft. They have very shallow but wide root systems that often link to the root systems of other trees.)
      Fauna: Three-Toed Sloth, Orangutans, Tree kangaroo, Opossum, Tree Frogs, Gibbons, Lemurs, Monkeys, Squirrels, Chameleons, Iguanas, Kinkajou, Anteater, Sun Bear
      Hostile Fauna: Angustus Crumens Carnivorous, Forceps Capitivus Carnivorous, Gum Hosta Carnivorous, Terra Anemone Carnivorous, Acidus Emanio Carnivorous, Chamelius Carnivorous, Venus Femina Forca Carnivorous, Slimewarticus Carnivorous, 

   (4) Upper Canopy (140ft to 180ft, 85% sunlight): Most of the upper canopy is connected to the lower canopy through vines and the upper limbs of trees in the lower canopy meeting the lower limbs of the trees here, but are otherwise unconnected to one another. The limbs of these trees are much smaller and spread out in a way that has a good deal of horizontal spread, but little vertical spread, creating a sort of umbrella like effect for the jungle below. The trees that reach to this height have very thick trunks and very dense wood but also hold a great deal of water & nutrients within them, making them the prime targets for parasitic vines. Within the leaves & branches of these trees are a large number of smaller arboreal animals, agile gliders, and very active carnivourous plants.
   "In the Upper Canopy, the jungle's communal spirit ends. Here, the great Samanea and Angelim Vermelho rise as solitary titans, their massive crowns spreading like golden-green shields against the sun. It is a world of vertical distance, where 'travel' means a death-defying leap or a precarious crawl along a wind-swept liana. The air is sharper here—hot, dry, and smelling of sun-baked wood—while the 'high-rise' trunks stand as silent, water-heavy sentinels over the chaos below."
      Environment: This layer experiences high, daily temperature fluctuations, high wind speeds, and direct sunlight, creating a "hot and dry" microclimate relative to the forest floor.
      Flora: African Baobab-Dipterocarp Hybrid, Angelim Vermelho, Samanea saman (Rain Tree), Brazilian Ironwood, Umbrella Catalpa, Umbrella Magnolia (These trees grow more slowely and develop deeper root systems than the fast growing lower canopy trees, but they also develop a much more dense canopy that collects more sunlight) 
      Fauna: Agile Gliders, Bats, Spider Monkey, Tree Squirrel, Flying Frog, Toucan, Woodpecker, Flying Snakes, Geckos, Koala, Sugar Glider
      Hostile Fauna: Acidus Emanio Carnivorous, Chamelius Carnivorous, Slimewarticus Carnivorous, 

   (5) High Canopy (200ft+, 100% sunlight): While the regions of the upper & lower canopy are highly interconnected, and creatures can easly move throughout the jungle within these two regions, the high canopy is virtually a world unto itself. The relatively thin, tall trees that reach above the jungle below secrete a slick, anti-epiphytic resin that makes it difficult for parasitic vines to attach to the trunk, so these trees have very few connections to the jungle below. Only within the high branches of these trees and some vines find home and grow downward to the upper canopy below. Within the high branches of the high canopy can be found thousands of birds and small arboreal animals that come up to steal their eggs.
   "The High Canopy is the jungle’s crown—a sparse, wind-scoured realm of 'Sovereign Spires.' The Meranti trunks rise like polished needles, coated in a shimmering, anti-epiphytic glaze that rejects the reach of the parasitic world below. Here, the 'Green Roar' of the lower layers fades into the whistle of high-altitude gales. It is a kingdom of light and feathers, where the trees live for centuries in solitary majesty, disconnected from the predatory hunger of the layers beneath them."
      Environment: Experiences high temperatures, strong winds, and high rainfall.
      Flora: Yellow Meranti, Ceiba (secrete a slick, anti-epiphytic resin)(These trees grow very slow, develop very deep root systems, and have a relatively small canopy that is very dense. They also have very long lifespans.)
      Fauna: Birds of all types
      Hostile Fauna: None

B. Shoreline/Wetlands/Swamps
"The Shoreline is a realm of salt, silt, and serrated edges. Here, the Great Stilt-Mangroves rise from the brackish tide like a forest of bleached bones, their roots interlocking into a jagged, impenetrable cage. The air is thick with the scent of brine and the sharp, metallic tang of 'Angler-Vines'—glistening, silk-thin threads that dangle from the canopy to 'fish' in the churning water below. It is a world of constant, rhythmic grinding. You can hear the low, tectonic rumble of the Reef-Crasher long before you see it; a massive, armored titan that carves jagged canyons through the root-mesh, leaving a wake of shattered wood and churning silt. In its path, the 'Net-Ferns' unfurl to feast on the debris, while the shimmering salt-crusted leaves above reflect the sun in a blinding, crystalline haze."

   (1) Root Zone (Submerged to 5ft): A complex maze of "prop roots" stretch downward from the trunk of the Cypress-Mangrove trees through the water, and into the soil below. While the trunks of the trees above may be around 5ft thick, the root system can stretch out as much as 10ft from the trunk before sinking into the soil. These "prop roots" often merge with the "prop roots" from other Cypress-Mangrove trees to create a complex maze of underwater roots stretching for miles. Large numbers of crabs, snails, and fish live within these root systems and many amphibians & semiaquatic creatures move through the area.
   "To enter the Root Zone is to move through a wooden ribcage. Below the waterline, the Cypress-Mangrove props form a sprawling, silt-choked labyrinth of petrified wood and sharp-edged oysters. The air here—trapped just inches above the tide—is heavy with the scent of brine and anaerobic mud. It is a world of constant, low-level friction: the scuttle of armored crabs across barnacle-encrusted bark and the rhythmic slosh of the brackish tide against the massive, 10-foot-wide root-buttresses. In the shadows of these 'salt-cages,' life is small, armored, and cautious, hiding from the gargantuan tremors of the Reef-Crasher that periodically turn this stable maze into a grinding graveyard of shattered timber."
      Flora: Cypress-Mangrove roots
      Fauna: Crabs, Snails, Fish, Turtles, Oysters, Sponges, Otters
      Hostile Fauna: None

   (2) Under Canopy (5ft to 40ft): A large number of epiphytes, vines, and climbing ferns anchor to the trees in this zone, as do many carnivorous plants that use lures to trap prey below.
   "The Shoreline Under-Canopy is a zone of precarious transit and 'Hanging Gallows.' Between the smooth, grey trunks of the Cypress-Mangroves, the air is a web of 'Angler-Vines' and 'Harpoon-Plants'—spectral, glistening threads that dangle like biological tripwires. There is an eerie stillness here, devoid of the frantic movement found in the jungle; it is a region of patient, predatory waiting. The only light is the shimmering reflection of the sun off the salt-crusted bark, casting 'ghost-patterns' that dance across the waxy leaves of the Terra Anemones. To move through this space is to navigate a field of silent, suspended mouths, where a single misstep into a lure means being reeled upward into the dark."
      Flora: Cypress-Mangrove trunks, Epiphytes, Vines, and Climbing Ferns
      Fauna: None, but many pass through
      Hostile Fauna: Terra Anemone Carnivorous, Harpoon-Plant

   (3) Lower Canopy (40ft to 100ft): A dense upper layer formed by the branches and leaves of Cypress-Mangrove trees forms a bast canopy in this zone, providing shelter for small arboreal animals, birds, bats, and insects. Smaller versions of many carnivorous plants found in the lower & upper canopies in the jungle can be found in within this zone.
   "The Lower Canopy is a 'Crystalline Bastion'—a thick, resilient ceiling of waxy needles and salt-hardened leaves. Unlike the breezy heights of the jungle, this layer feels fortified. The interlocking branches of the Cypress-Mangroves create a dense, vibrating theater of life, where the screech of monkeys and the hum of salt-flies are constant. Everything here is encrusted in a fine, white shimmer; the trees exude salt like a protective frost, reflecting the blinding sun in a haze that stings the eyes. It is a busy, competitive hub where the 'Mawhives' nest in the crooks of heavy limbs, feasting on the insects that thrive in the humid heat trapped beneath the emerald shield."
      Flora: Cypress-Mangroves, Epiphytes, Vines
      Fauna: Small arboreal animals (monkeys and racoons), birds, bats, and insects
      Hostile Fauna: Slimewarticus Carnivorous, Terra Anemone Carnivorous, Mawhive, Angler-Plant

   (4) Upper Canopy (100ft to 200ft): Occasional Spire-Redwoods emerge from the vast Cypress-Mangrove forests, reaching around twice their height. While few carnivorous plants live within this zone, a large number of avians make their home here, most notably the fisher birds (four winged Pelicans).
   "The Upper Canopy is a realm of lonely, wind-scoured majesty. Here, the Spire-Redwoods pierce the green Bastion like copper needles, surging 200 feet into the unfiltered glare of the sun. It is a world of incredible height and terrifying silence, disconnected from the predatory struggle of the mud below. The air is sharp and tastes of the open sea, carrying the calls of the 'Four-Winged Pelicans' that wheel around the conical crowns. These towers are the shoreline's watchpoints—isolated 'sky-islands' where the only connection to the world below is the occasional, wind-whipped liana and the distant, tectonic boom of the sea crashing against the reef far on the horizon."
      Flora: Spire-Redwoods, Epiphytes, Vines
      Fauna: Birds of all types
      Hostile Fauna: None

Cypress-Mangrove Trees: The root system starts high up the trunk (the "stilt" phase) and arches outward in a series of 15-foot-high flying buttresses. This creates a literal vaulted basement above the waterline where the Reef-Crasher can actually move between the roots of the larger trees. Instead of broad leaves, the canopy consists of thick, waxy needles that exude salt. In the morning, the trees appear to be covered in shimmering white "shards," which fall off as the wind picks up, creating a constant "salt-snow" on the water's surface.

Spire-Redwood (Metasequoia): Unlike the Cypress-Mangroves that interlock their roots, the Redwoods stand alone. Their trunks are massive, smooth, and copper-bronzed—rising like perfectly turned columns. They stand around 200ft feet high, dwarfing the 100-foot Cypress-Mangroves. To survive the saline and unstable mud, the Redwood creates a massive, conical, and calcified base. It looks less like wood and more like a rugged, grey-white coral mound that acts as an independent breakwater. Because the Redwoods stand so high and isolated, the area directly around their base (between their canopy and the lower Cypress-Mangrove ceiling) is an "air void." It is a clear, wind-swept zone that breaks the density of the surrounding swamp.

Angler-Plant
Growing in the branches of trees over the water, this plant drops thin, high-tensile threads made of a sticky, protein-based glue (similar to spider silk but much thicker). These hang inches above or just below the water's surface. At the end of these threads, the plant grows a small, bioluminescent bulb or a fleshy "mimic" that looks like a local insect. When a creature jumps or swims to grab the lure, the thread retracts with incredible speed, pulling the prey up into a digestive "pitcher" nested in the high branches. The tree doesn't have eyes; it "feels" the ripples in the water through its low-hanging "sensory roots" and drops its lines only when it detects movement.

Harpoon-Plant
Growing on the trunks of trees leaning over the water, these plants have pressurized, fluid-filled chambers. When a creature breaks the surface of the water, the plant fires a biomineralized "dart" attached to a vine. The dart is barbed and often coated in a paralytic toxin. Once the prey is "hooked," the plant winds the vine back in using a ratcheting growth mechanism.


C. Shallow Seas
   (1) Coral Reefs (0ft to -100 ft). Known as the "rainforests of the sea," these biodiversity hotspots are formed by living colonies of specialized animals that require clear, shallow water to grow, supporting 25% of all marine species.
   (2) Seagrass Meadows (-5ft to -50ft). These habitats are composed of flowering plants, often in shallow bays, lagoons, and sandy shallow regions throughout the shallow seas of Pandora. Many types of aquatic fauna can be found within these areas, far too many to even begin to list.
   (3) Kelp Forests (-50ft to -150ft). These densely packed areas of large, seaweed-like algae grow on rocky seabeds and are found in many coastal regions. These areas are home to Leafy Seadragon, young deep water fish, flying squid, Seahorses, Pipefish, Snappers, Groupers, Cuttlefish, Spiny Lobsters, Moray Eels, Boxfish, Cowfish, and Sea Turtles and is often visited by otters.
   (4) Grassy Plateaus (-150ft to -250ft). Most of the deeper regions of the shallow seas are sandy areas that harbor Deep-Water Seagrass, Red Algae, and occasional Deep-Water Corals. These areas are home to Crustaceans, Manatees, Dugongs, Giant Sea Slugs, Stargazers, Flatfish, and Long-Spined Sea Urchins and are often visted by larger sea mammels and fish.
   (5) Lilypad Islands (-250ft to -300ft). Far offshore, these large 'Lilypads' grow up to the surafce from roots hundreds of feet deep. The upper, floating "Lillypad" portion of these plants is normally 60 to 100 feet in diamater and around 3ft deep. The "Lillypad" is connected to the roots deep below with a long, thick Petiole-Stipe tether which transports nutrients and air between the surface and the roots. The roots themselves grow on the edges of deep canyons & cliffs and cover large areas with wood-like anchors. A large number of plants and animals live on and around both the upper "Lillypad" and the roots.
   (6) Thermal Spires (-300ft to -600ft). Deep underwater, in and around the many tectonic plates of the world can be found thermal vents. In some regions these thermal vents are clustered in such a way as they form their own ecosystems. Those found in the deeper areas of the shallow seas have just enough light for some photosynthesis, which combined with mineral rich thermal vents can create a vibrant underwater ecosystem.
